      <!-- SECTION 1 & 2: WHAT IS VOLUME -->
      <div
        class="space-y-4 border-t border-slate-200/80 pt-6 dark:border-slate-800"
      >
        <h2
          class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2"
        >
          <span>📐</span> 1. What is Volume and How is It Calculated?
        </h2>
        <p
          class="text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300"
        >
          In physics and geometry, <strong>volume</strong> is the measure of the three-dimensional
          space enclosed by a closed boundary or occupied by a substance (solid, liquid,
          or gas). While surface area measures the boundary outside of an object in
          two dimensions (units squared), volume quantifies what fits inside (units
          cubed).
        </p>
        <p
          class="text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300"
        >
          The International System of Units (SI) defines the <strong
            >cubic meter (m³)</strong
          > as the base unit of volume. In daily practice, trade, and engineering,
          derived units like <strong>liters (L)</strong>, <strong
            >US liquid gallons</strong
          >, <strong>Imperial gallons</strong>, and <strong
            >cubic feet (ft³)</strong
          > are frequently preferred depending on whether liquid storage, civil construction,
          or HVAC ductwork is being sized.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 3: TANK VOLUME -->
      <div
        class="space-y-4 border-t border-slate-200/80 pt-6 dark:border-slate-800"
      >
        <h2
          class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2"
        >
          <span>📦</span> 2. Tank Volume Calculator (Rectangular &amp; Cylindrical
          Tanks)
        </h2>
        <p
          class="text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300"
        >
          Water tanks, sump pits, fuel reservoirs, and shipping containers
          generally take the form of either a rectangular prism (box) or a
          cylinder.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div
            class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800/40"
          >
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-1.5">
              Rectangular Tank Formula
            </h3>
            <p
              class="text-xs font-mono text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 mb-2 font-bold"
            >
              V = Length × Width × Height
            </p>
            <p
              class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed"
            >
              Multiplying length, width, and height in meters gives volume in
              m³. To find liquid capacity in liters, multiply the m³ result by
              1,000 (since 1 m³ = 1,000 Liters).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800/40"
          >
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-1.5">
              Vertical Cylindrical Tank Formula
            </h3>
            <p
              class="text-xs font-mono text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 mb-2 font-bold"
            >
              V = π × r² × h = π × (d / 2)² × h
            </p>
            <p
              class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed"
            >
              For standing drums, silos, and overhead Sintex-type tanks,
              calculate the cross-sectional circular area (πr²) and multiply by
              liquid height.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

