  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How do I calculate volume in general?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          In general, volume is calculated by multiplying the cross-sectional
          base area of a 3D object by its height. For a box, Area = Length ×
          Width, so Volume = Length × Width × Height. For a cylinder, Base Area
          = πr², so Volume = πr²h.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How many liters are in 1 cubic meter?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          There are exactly <strong>1,000 Liters</strong> in 1 cubic meter (1 m³ =
          1,000 L). Because 1 Liter equals 1 cubic decimeter (10 cm × 10 cm × 10 cm
          = 1,000 cm³), exactly 1,000 one-liter cubes fit inside a 1-meter cube.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How do I calculate tank volume and water capacity?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          For a rectangular water tank, measure the internal length, width, and
          height in meters. Multiply them together to obtain cubic meters, then
          multiply by 1,000 to get total liquid capacity in liters. For example,
          a 5m × 2m × 1.5m tank holds 15 m³ = 15,000 Liters.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How do I calculate cylinder volume?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Use the formula <strong>V = π × r² × h</strong>. If you know the
          diameter (d), divide it by 2 to get the radius (r = d / 2), square the
          radius, multiply by π (approximately 3.14159), and multiply by the
          cylinder height.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>Why is internal diameter required for pipe capacity?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          A pipe's walls have physical thickness. Water flows only through the
          inner hollow space (the lumen). Calculating capacity using outer
          diameter significantly overstates water capacity. If you have outer
          diameter, subtract twice the wall thickness: <code
            >ID = OD − (2 × Wall Thickness)</code
          >.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How do I calculate swimming pool volume with a slope?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Calculate the average depth by adding the shallow end depth to the
          deep end depth and dividing by 2: <code
            >Avg Depth = (Shallow + Deep) / 2</code
          >. Then multiply by length and width: <code
            >Volume = Length × Width × Average Depth</code
          >.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How do I calculate sphere volume?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The volume of a sphere is <strong>(4 / 3) × π × r³</strong>. Cube the
          radius (r × r × r), multiply by π, and multiply by 4/3. For a 5 cm
          radius sphere, (4/3) × π × 125 ≈ 523.6 cm³ (approx 0.52 Liters).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How do I calculate cone volume?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The volume of a cone is exactly one-third the volume of a cylinder
          with the same dimensions: <strong>V = (1 / 3) × π × r² × h</strong>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How is prostate volume estimated clinically?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Urologists use the prolate ellipsoid formula: <code
            >Length (cm) × Width (cm) × Height (cm) × 0.52</code
          >. The 0.52 factor approximates π/6. The result is expressed in mL or
          cc.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details
        class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
      >
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer"
        >
          <span>How do I convert cubic meters to US gallons?</span>
          <span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Multiply cubic meters by <strong>264.172</strong>. For example, 10 m³
          of water equals approximately 2,641.72 US gallons.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
